title: '7 Steps to Get Your Business Website Found on Google'
excerpt: 'A beginner-friendly SEO guide for small businesses: from Google Search Console and Google Business Profile to site speed and content that answers customer questions.'
date: 2026-11-20
category: SEO
draft: false
---
Your website is live, but it never shows up when people search for your business or services? You're not alone. A website that's online is **not automatically** found on Google — it has to be introduced and set up properly. Here are seven fundamental steps you can start today.

## 1. Add your website to Google Search Console

[Google Search Console](https://search.google.com/search-console) is a free Google tool for monitoring how your site appears in search results. Once your site is verified, submit your **sitemap** (usually at `/sitemap.xml`) so Google knows which pages exist.

It also shows which search terms bring people to your website and flags pages with problems.

## 2. Create and complete your Google Business Profile

For local businesses, this is often the step with the fastest impact. [Google Business Profile](https://www.google.com/business/) puts your business on Google Maps and in the panel beside search results.

Fill in everything: business category, opening hours, WhatsApp number, address, real photos, and a **link to your website**. Ask happy customers to leave a review — genuine reviews help new customers trust you.

## 3. One page per topic

Google matches pages to searches. If you offer AC servicing and CCTV installation, create a **separate page** for each instead of mentioning both in one paragraph on your homepage.

Every page should have:

- A clear **title tag**, for example "AC Service in Bekasi — Business Name"
- A short **meta description** that invites the click
- **One main heading (H1)** that describes the page

## 4. Make sure the site is fast and mobile-friendly

Most visitors to small business websites come from smartphones, and Google primarily evaluates the mobile version of your site. Check your site with [PageSpeed Insights](https://pagespeed.web.dev/) and pay attention to:

- Compressing images and using modern formats such as WebP
- Removing unnecessary plugins and scripts
- Buttons large enough to tap and text readable without zooming

## 5. Write content that answers customer questions

Think about the questions customers ask on WhatsApp: "How much is it?", "How long does it take?", "Do you serve other cities?". Each question can become an **FAQ entry or a blog post**.

This kind of content does two jobs: it gives Google a reason to show your website, and it answers prospects' questions before they contact you.

## 6. Use the words your customers use

Customers rarely search with technical terms. They type things like "affordable website design" or "boxed catering Bekasi". Use those words **naturally** in your titles, headings, and page copy — without repeating them unnaturally.

Include your city or service area if your business serves specific locations.

## 7. Earn links from other websites

Links from other websites (backlinks) signal that your business is trusted. For small businesses, start with the realistic ones:

- Local business directories and industry associations
- Partners, suppliers, or clients willing to mention you
- Local media or community coverage
- Social media profiles that link to your website

Avoid buying large numbers of backlinks from irrelevant sites — it can do more harm than good.

## Be patient, then measure

SEO isn't instant. For a new website, results usually start to show after a few weeks to a few months. Track progress in Search Console, add content regularly, and improve pages that aren't performing yet.

> A website that ranks on Google is a salesperson working around the clock, with no cost per click.

## Conclusion

Start with the foundations: Search Console, Google Business Profile, a clear page structure, and a fast website. After that, content that answers customer questions and links from other websites will bring visitors consistently.

